{:schema 1, :paired-ratio {:ic02 {:p50-ms 17.837481698389457, :p95-ms 47.78160366719591, :p99-ms 47.78160366719591, :mean-ms 18.356240606427534, :samples 15}, :ic07 {:p50-ms 2.4223574134569614, :p95-ms 41.12816937972125, :p99-ms 41.12816937972125, :mean-ms 6.987700796889986, :samples 15}, :ic08 {:p50-ms 12.599041377286511, :p95-ms 66.20752206096935, :p99-ms 66.20752206096935, :mean-ms 19.64189809268107, :samples 15}, :ic09 {:p50-ms 12.843546958138859, :p95-ms 32.99621734744751, :p99-ms 32.99621734744751, :mean-ms 13.958052313636237, :samples 15}, :note "kotobase-ms / neo4j-ms computed PER SAMPLE with the two engines run back to back, then summarised. This is the column to compare across runs; a quotient of two separately-summarised medians is not, which is what every receipt before 2026-08-02 used."}, :caveats ["Subset of SF1, not full SF1 (~10.5M elements) -- the subset rule is stated above and is deterministic." "kotobase has no ORDER BY/LIMIT in its query language; the top-20 step runs in harness Clojure over the join's full result, while Neo4j does it inside Cypher. Both are timed to the same final answer and the answers are asserted equal." "kotobase stores knows in both directions (no undirected edge) and reifies likes (no edge properties); Neo4j uses a native undirected match and a relationship property." "kotobase's number is a hot in-memory db with no encryption and no persistence; the persisted path additionally pays hydration, which is measured in ADR-2607310900 and the dag-shape receipt, not here." "Neo4j embedded has no pure in-memory mode; its temp on-disk store is read through the page cache after warmup." "Single machine, single JVM, no concurrency. NamiDB's published numbers are on different hardware -- only the ratio against the shared Neo4j yardstick is comparable, and only loosely." "IC07/IC09 agreement compares result SETS (likers / message ids) rather than ordered rows: both queries can have creationDate ties at the LIMIT-20 boundary, where the two engines are free to pick different rows. IC02/IC08 are compared as ordered rows. A reported disagreement is reported, never relaxed after the fact."], :kotobase {:quads 409877, :build-ms 2203.13675, :note "hot db via eng/transact -- same db value the persisted path hydrates to; no AES-GCM/content-addressing in this number", :ic02 {:p50-ms 99.059917, :p95-ms 246.777458, :p99-ms 246.777458, :mean-ms 122.98004440000001, :samples 15}, :ic07 {:p50-ms 2.726708, :p95-ms 48.320458, :p99-ms 48.320458, :mean-ms 13.3678082, :samples 15}, :ic08 {:p50-ms 25.421917, :p95-ms 196.468041, :p99-ms 196.468041, :mean-ms 37.96292213333333, :samples 15}, :ic09 {:p50-ms 786.314833, :p95-ms 1422.945375, :p99-ms 1422.945375, :mean-ms 890.8748584000001, :samples 15}}, :receipt/type :ldbc-snb-interactive, :answers-agree {:ic02 true, :ic07 true, :ic08 true, :ic09 true}, :implementation "kotobase-peer.ldbc-snb-bench", :dataset {:name "LDBC SNB Interactive v1, SF1, Datagen v1.0.0, CsvComposite/LongDateFormatter", :source "https://repository.surfsara.nl/datasets/cwi/ldbc-snb-interactive-v1-datagen-v100", :subset-rule "all persons + all knows; 3000 most recent Posts + full transitive reply trees; hasCreator for included Messages; likes onto included Messages", :elements {:comments 4355, :reply-of 4355, :likes 809, :persons 9892, :total 210389, :messages 7355, :knows-edges 180623, :has-creator 7355, :posts 3000}, :load-ms 24304.859042}, :neo4j-embedded {:load-ms 14573.733916, :version "org.neo4j/neo4j 2025.03.0 embedded (community), temp on-disk store, pid UNIQUE constraints awaited online", :ic02 {:p50-ms 5.8055, :p95-ms 151.782916, :p99-ms 151.782916, :mean-ms 17.451988800000002, :samples 15}, :ic07 {:p50-ms 1.213416, :p95-ms 167.009959, :p99-ms 167.009959, :mean-ms 14.183138866666663, :samples 15}, :ic08 {:p50-ms 1.390625, :p95-ms 155.5505, :p99-ms 155.5505, :mean-ms 14.718469333333335, :samples 15}, :ic09 {:p50-ms 55.254166, :p95-ms 161.784334, :p99-ms 161.784334, :mean-ms 75.34412486666665, :samples 15}}, :start-persons {:n 15, :rule "highest knows-degree, sorted by id"}
 :what-changed-in-the-harness
 "The two engines were run SEQUENTIALLY -- all 15 kotobase samples, then all 15 Neo4j samples -- while every receipt presented the kb/neo ratio as the load-invariant column precisely because both were supposed to see the same conditions. They did not. Now each start person is measured on both engines back to back, and the ratio is taken PER SAMPLE before being summarised."

 :the-fix-validates-itself
 "Under the sequential harness the paired ratio and the quotient-of-medians disagreed badly: across two runs of byte-identical kotobase code, IC07 read 4.7x then 13.9x (2026-08-02-limit-pushdown-third-attempt.edn). Interleaved, the two ways of computing it now agree within ~1.5x on every query -- paired 17.8 / 2.4 / 12.6 / 12.8 against quotients 17.1 / 2.2 / 18.3 / 14.2. Agreement between two estimators that disagreed before is the evidence the instrument was the problem."

 :this-corrects-numbers-i-published
 {:note "Same arrangement main, no B-2. These are what the queries actually cost relative to Neo4j; the earlier figures were taken on the broken instrument."
  :ic09 {:earlier-claims [85.3 35.8 21.6] :measured-now 12.8}
  :ic08 {:earlier-claims [97.5 53.3 38.7] :measured-now 12.6}
  :ic07 {:earlier-claims [6.2 7.0 6.0] :measured-now 2.4}
  :ic02 {:earlier-claims [7.7 14.7 19.2] :measured-now 17.8}
  :reading "IC08 and IC09 were reported roughly 3x worse than they are. IC02 was reported better early and worse later; 17.8x is the number. The DIRECTION of the join work remains -- IC09 and IC08 did improve substantially across the session -- but the magnitudes in every ratio receipt before this one were measured with the two engines in different minutes."}

